{"title": "CSCI-549: Seminar in Languages and Tools", "learningObjectives": [
    "Define the structural and semantic components of a programming language reference.",
    "Explain how compiler phases separate parsing, verification, and code generation.",
    "Apply evaluation criteria to assess faculty-proposed seminar courses in the Languages and Tools cluster."
  ], "beats": [
    {
      "title": "Course Scope and Faculty Pilot Framework",
      "claim": "CSCI-549 serves as a faculty pilot course to examine current topics in Languages and Tools.",
      "example": "A department head proposes a new undergraduate offering focused on compiler design.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a flowchart showing faculty proposal -> pilot course -> cluster classification (Languages and Tools or Security).",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-549"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Cluster Classification System",
      "claim": "Specific course instances are identified as belonging to the Languages and Tools cluster, the Security cluster, or both clusters.",
      "example": "A proposed seminar on memory safety analysis is classified under both clusters.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a Venn diagram with two overlapping circles labeled 'Languages and Tools' and 'Security', with a course title in the intersection.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-549"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Language Reference Structure",
      "claim": "The Python language reference specifies lexical analysis, data model behavior, execution, expressions, statements, functions, classes, exceptions, and module import semantics.",
      "example": "A reference document outlines how Python handles variable binding and exception propagation.",
      "visualDirection": "List the specified reference components in a hierarchical outline format.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Object Model and Namespaces",
      "claim": "Python objects have identity, a type, and a value, while names are bound to objects within namespaces and scopes.",
      "example": "A function call binds the parameter name to a specific object instance in the local scope.",
      "visualDirection": "Illustrate a namespace tree with nested scopes and object references pointing to shared memory addresses.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Control Flow and Exceptions",
      "claim": "Expressions produce values, while statements control execution and bindings, and exceptions interrupt normal control flow until a matching handler processes them or they propagate.",
      "example": "A division by zero triggers an exception that jumps to a try-except block.",
      "visualDirection": "Draw a control flow graph with a normal path and an exception path branching to a handler node.",
      "sourceIds": ["python-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "LLVM Intermediate Representation",
      "claim": "LLVM IR is a typed, SSA-based intermediate representation with explicit instructions, basic blocks, functions, memory operations, and control flow.",
      "example": "A compiler translates a high-level loop into a sequence of LLVM IR basic blocks with phi nodes.",
      "visualDirection": "Render a simplified LLVM IR module showing a function, basic blocks, and a phi instruction at a merge point.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Phi Instructions and Control Flow",
      "claim": "Phi instructions select values according to the predecessor control-flow edge, ensuring single static assignment form is maintained.",
      "example": "A phi node chooses between two loop iteration values based on whether the loop condition was true or false.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a control flow graph with two incoming edges to a block containing a phi instruction that selects between two values.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Memory Operations and Verification",
      "claim": "Load and store instructions make memory access explicit, and valid IR must satisfy structural and typing rules, with the verifier reporting IR that violates those rules.",
      "example": "The verifier rejects an IR module where a store instruction targets an untyped memory location.",
      "visualDirection": "Display a before-and-after comparison of invalid IR (with a type mismatch) and valid IR (with explicit memory types).",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Compiler Phase Separation",
      "claim": "Compiler work separates parsing and semantic analysis from lowering, verification, optimization, and target-specific code generation.",
      "example": "A frontend parser generates ASTs, while a backend optimizer restructures the IR for a specific CPU architecture.",
      "visualDirection": "Present a linear pipeline diagram with distinct stages: Parsing -> Semantic Analysis -> Lowering -> Verification -> Optimization -> Code Generation.",
      "sourceIds": ["llvm-language-reference"]
    },
    {
      "title": "Practical Analysis Checklist",
      "claim": "Faculty must evaluate proposed seminar courses against stable process standards, planning methods, and assessment criteria to ensure alignment with the Languages and Tools cluster.",
      "example": "A review checklist verifies that the proposed course includes defined learning outcomes, assessment methods, and resource needs.",
      "visualDirection": "Show a checklist template with checkboxes for prerequisites, format, learning outcomes, assessment methods, and resource needs.",
      "sourceIds": ["rit-csci-549"]
    }
  ]}
